# A187 — the CI's tool downloads are verified now (2026-09-16)

## What the workflow did
# `.github/workflows/checks.yml:133-142` piped each release asset straight into `tar`:
#     curl -fsSL "https://github.com/koalaman/shellcheck/releases/download/v${SHELLCHECK_VERSION}/…" \
#       | tar -xJ -C "$tools"
# A version-pinned URL is not integrity: a GitHub release asset can be deleted and re-uploaded by
# anyone with release rights on that repository, and the job then runs whatever is there. The same
# repository's `tools/fetch-metal.sh` pins a SHA-256 for its own download, and the workflow's header
# claimed the tools were pinned.

## The digests, from two sources
# GitHub's releases API publishes a `digest` per asset; each was then checked byte-for-byte against the
# downloaded file (`shasum -a 256 -c -`). Both figures agree, and the sizes match the API's `size`.

asset                                  API digest (sha256)                                                size        local check
shellcheck-v0.11.0.linux.x86_64.tar.xz 8c3be12b05d5c177a04c29e3c78ce89ac86f1595681cab149b65b97c4e227198   2 559 196   OK
gitleaks_8.30.1_linux_x64.tar.gz       551f6fc83ea457d62a0d98237cbad105af8d557003051f41f3e7ca7b3f2470eb   8 230 402   OK
osv-scanner_linux_amd64                f9f25499a2c8cc367b3af45df2ea7eeca7fbccceab9c35079968f4b3652194be  57 725 090   OK

# Where the API numbers come from, so the next version bump does not have to trust this file:
#   curl -fsSL "https://api.github.com/repos/<owner>/<repo>/releases/tags/v<version>" \
#     | python3 -c 'import json,sys; [print(a["name"], a.get("digest")) for a in json.load(sys.stdin)["assets"]]'

## The fix
# `tools/fetch-audit-tools.sh` downloads, verifies and unpacks the three, and the workflow calls it:
#     bash tools/fetch-audit-tools.sh --into "$tools/bin"
# An empty pin is an error rather than "no verification needed", which is the rule A24 set for
# `fetch-metal.sh`. The Python and Node tools are deliberately not in the script: PyPI and npm refuse
# to publish a version twice, so for `ruff==0.16.7` and `pyright@1.1.414` the version *is* the pin.

## It is measured, not asserted
$ bash tools/fetch-audit-tools.sh --verify-only          # on node1 (macOS; downloads and hashes only)
  …/shellcheck-v0.11.0.linux.x86_64.tar.xz: OK
  shellcheck 0.11.0 verified
  …/gitleaks_8.30.1_linux_x64.tar.gz: OK
  gitleaks 8.30.1 verified
  …/osv-scanner: OK
  osv-scanner 2.5.1 verified

# With one character changed in a pinned digest — the mutation a rule like this exists to catch:
  …/shellcheck-v0.11.0.linux.x86_64.tar.xz: FAILED
  sha256sum: WARNING: 1 computed checksum did NOT match
  exit 1

# With a pin emptied:
  error: no SHA-256 pinned for …/gitleaks_8.30.1_linux_x64.tar.gz
  exit 1

# Usage errors, which are how a script this size goes wrong quietly:
$ bash tools/fetch-audit-tools.sh
  error: --into <dir> is required (or use --verify-only)          exit 2
$ bash tools/fetch-audit-tools.sh --into /tmp/x                   # on macOS
  error: these are Linux binaries and this is Darwin; use --verify-only   exit 2

## The version record cannot drift now
# The workflow installs what `tools/fetch-audit-tools.sh` pins, and `AUDIT/environment.md` records what
# the audit ran. A step compares them, so a version bumped in only one place fails the job:
$ (the check's own loop, run on node1)
  shellcheck   script 0.11.0   environment.md 0.11.0
  gitleaks     script 8.30.1   environment.md 8.30.1
  osv-scanner  script 2.5.1    environment.md 2.5.1
  versions agree
# and with the script at 8.30.0 against the record's 8.30.1: DISAGREE, exit 1.

## Locally
$ bash -n tools/fetch-audit-tools.sh && shellcheck -S style tools/fetch-audit-tools.sh
  (clean; the first version tripped SC1073 because a comment line began with "shellcheck-" and
   shellcheck read it as one of its own directives)
# The workflow parses as YAML (`python3 -c 'import yaml; yaml.safe_load(...)'`), with the new check
# listed among the generated-files steps.
